{
  "reference_answer": "Teamwork is useful, but I'd say its real value shows up when a problem is too messy for one person to even define properly. If you're stuck on your own, you can go round in circles for hours without noticing you're asking the wrong question. In a team, someone usually says something that makes you stop and rethink. That's not about splitting tasks or covering weaknesses — it's about the thinking itself getting sharper because other people poke holes in it. Of course, this only works if people actually speak up. If everyone just agrees with the loudest voice, a team can be slower and more confident than one person, which is a bad combination. So teamwork is useful mainly when it forces you to test your own assumptions.",
  "reference_angle_summary": "独立主线：团队合作的价值在于通过他人质疑来修正和深化个人思考，而非分工或互补短板；示例不代表用户经历。",
  "takeaway_expressions": [
    {
      "expression": "go round in circles",
      "meaning_zh": "原地打转，反复讨论却没有进展",
      "why_useful": "描述低效思考或讨论时非常自然，口语中高频出现",
      "example": "We kept going round in circles until someone asked a simpler question.",
      "usage_note": "常与 keep 或 go 搭配，主语通常是人或讨论"
    },
    {
      "expression": "poke holes in it",
      "meaning_zh": "挑出漏洞，提出质疑",
      "why_useful": "形象表达批判性讨论，比 criticize 更口语化",
      "example": "Her job was to poke holes in our plan before we presented it.",
      "usage_note": "后面常接 idea, plan, argument 等"
    },
    {
      "expression": "the loudest voice",
      "meaning_zh": "声音最大的人，指最有话语权或最强势的人",
      "why_useful": "讨论群体决策、会议动态时很实用",
      "example": "In some meetings, everyone just follows the loudest voice.",
      "usage_note": "常与 follow, agree with 等搭配"
    }
  ]
}
